<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sugita Genpaku</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1733–1817</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sugita Genpaku was a Japanese physician and scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangaku">rangaku</a>, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> learning. After watching a dissection in Edo in 1771 that confirmed the accuracy of a Dutch anatomy book, he and his colleagues translated it as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaitai-shinsho">Kaitai Shinsho</a>, published in 1774. His memoir, Rangaku Kotohajime, describes the difficulties of that work and became the founding story of Western learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sugita-genpaku.jpg" alt="Sugita Genpaku" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ishikawa Tairō · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sugita_Genpaku.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
